  <PageHero title="Tibet Travel Tips" subtitle="Altitude survival, complete packing list, health and safety advice, travel insurance guide, and everything you need to prepare for Lhasa." breadcrumb="Travel Tips" updated="August 2026" />

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-6 py-10 md:py-14">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="altitude">Altitude: The Science and the Survival Guide</h2>
      <p>Lhasa sits at 3,650 meters (11,975 feet). At this elevation, the air contains approximately 40% less oxygen than at sea level. Your body needs time to adapt by producing more red blood cells and increasing your breathing rate -- a process called acclimatization that takes 24-72 hours.</p>
      <p><strong>Symptoms to expect:</strong> Mild headache, fatigue after minimal exertion, shortness of breath when walking uphill or climbing stairs, disrupted sleep, and reduced appetite. These are normal and not dangerous in themselves.</p>
      <p><strong>Warning signs that require immediate action:</strong> Severe headache unresponsive to painkillers, confusion or difficulty walking in a straight line, persistent dry cough with pink or frothy sputum, and extreme shortness of breath at rest. These may indicate High Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE) or High Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE), which are life-threatening emergencies. Descend immediately and seek medical help.</p>
      
      <h3>The Acclimatization Strategy</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Fly to a lower city first.</strong> Spend 2-3 days in Chengdu (500m), Xi'an (400m), or Kunming (1,900m) before flying to Lhasa. This partial acclimatization makes a significant difference.</li>
        <li><strong>Take the train.</strong> The Qinghai-Tibet Railway from Xining (2,275m) to Lhasa takes approximately 21 hours. The gradual ascent and pressurized, oxygen-enriched train cars help your body adjust.</li>
        <li><strong>Rest completely on arrival day.</strong> Do not plan any sightseeing. Do not even walk around aggressively. Lie down, drink water, eat lightly, and let your body do its work.</li>
        <li><strong>Hydrate relentlessly.</strong> At altitude, you lose water through increased respiration and the dry air. Aim for 3-4 liters per day. Your urine should be clear or pale yellow.</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid alcohol and sedatives.</strong> Both depress your respiratory drive, which is the last thing you want when your body is already starved for oxygen.</li>
        <li><strong>Climb high, sleep low.</strong> If visiting higher areas outside Lhasa, return to a lower elevation to sleep whenever possible.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Medication</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Acetazolamide (Diamox):</strong> The most proven altitude sickness prevention medication. It works by acidifying your blood, which stimulates breathing. Start 125-250mg twice daily, 24 hours before ascending. Side effects include tingling in fingers and toes and increased urination (both normal).</li>
        <li><strong>Ibuprofen:</strong> Effective for altitude headaches. Take 400mg as needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Dexamethasone:</strong> A powerful steroid used for treating severe altitude sickness. Requires a prescription. Discuss with your doctor before travel.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Complete Packing List</h2>
      <h3>Clothing (Layers are Everything)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Warm insulated jacket (down or synthetic, rated to at least -5 degrees C for spring/autumn, -20 for winter)</li>
        <li>Thermal base layers (merino wool preferred -- no cotton)</li>
        <li>Mid-layer fleece or wool sweater</li>
        <li>Comfortable walking shoes with good grip (the Potala steps are uneven stone)</li>
        <li>Warm socks (2-3 pairs of wool or synthetic)</li>
        <li>Sun hat with a brim</li>
        <li>Warm beanie for evenings and early mornings</li>
        <li>Light gloves (even in summer, mornings are cold)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Sun and Health Protection</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>SPF 50+ broad-spectrum sunscreen (the UV index at 3,700m can exceed 12 in summer)</li>
        <li>UV-protective sunglasses (category 3 or 4 -- the glare off white palace walls at altitude is intense)</li>
        <li>SPF 30+ lip balm</li>
        <li>Personal medications plus prescription copies</li>
        <li>Basic first aid: bandaids, antiseptic wipes, blister pads, anti-diarrheal medication</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Documents and Essentials</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Passport (6+ months validity)</li>
        <li>Printed copies of Chinese visa and Tibet Travel Permit (at least 3 copies each)</li>
        <li>Travel insurance documents with emergency contact numbers</li>
        <li>Cash in RMB (ATMs are available in Lhasa but less reliable outside the city center)</li>
        <li>Power bank (phone batteries drain faster in cold)</li>
        <li>Reusable water bottle (1L minimum)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Travel Insurance: Read the Fine Print</h2>
      <p>This is not optional for Tibet. Standard travel insurance policies often contain a high-altitude exclusion -- typically excluding coverage above 2,500-3,000 meters. Before purchasing, verify that your policy:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Explicitly covers travel above 3,700 meters</li>
        <li>Includes emergency medical evacuation (helicopter evacuation from remote areas of Tibet can cost USD 15,000-50,000)</li>
        <li>Covers trip cancellation due to permit denial (rare but possible)</li>
        <li>Covers pre-existing conditions if relevant</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Reputable providers for high-altitude travel insurance include World Nomads (Explorer plan), IMG Global, and Allianz (with the adventure sports add-on). Check current policies as offerings change annually.</p>

      <h2>Health and Safety in Lhasa</h2>

      <h2>Money, SIM Cards, and Staying Connected</h2>
      <h3>Currency and Payments</h3>
      <p>China uses the Renminbi (RMB/CNY). In Lhasa, cash is still widely used, especially at small restaurants, market stalls, and monastery entrances. ATMs accepting foreign cards are available at the Bank of China on Beijing East Road and near Barkhor, but they occasionally run out of cash or reject foreign cards. Carry enough cash for 2-3 days at all times.</p>
      <p>Mobile payments (WeChat Pay and Alipay) dominate in China. Foreign visitors can now link international credit cards to these apps — set this up before arriving. Many small vendors in Lhasa only accept mobile payments, so this is worth the setup effort.</p>

      <h3>SIM Cards and Internet</h3>
      <p>Your foreign SIM card will work in Lhasa via roaming, but at high cost. A local China Mobile or China Unicom SIM card costs approximately CNY 50-100 with a data plan. You will need your passport to purchase one. Major carriers have shops on Beijing East Road. Set up a VPN on your phone before arriving in China — many international websites and apps (Google, WhatsApp, Instagram, Gmail) are blocked without one. Test your VPN before you leave home; some stop working once inside China.</p>

      <h3>WiFi</h3>
      <p>Most mid-range and luxury hotels in Lhasa offer WiFi, but speeds vary widely and international sites will be blocked unless you use a VPN. Do not rely on hotel WiFi for anything critical. Download offline maps (Maps.me or Google Maps offline mode), translation apps, and any reference materials before arriving.</p>

      <h2>Cultural Etiquette in Tibet</h2>
      <p>Tibetan culture is warm, deeply religious, and governed by specific customs that visitors should respect:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Walk clockwise.</strong> When visiting monasteries, temples, or circumambulation paths (like the Barkhor circuit), always walk clockwise around sacred objects, prayer wheels, and religious buildings. This is the direction of spiritual merit.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not touch or point at religious objects.</strong> Pointing at Buddha statues, thangkas, or monks is considered disrespectful. If you need to indicate something, use an open palm with all fingers extended, gesturing upward.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask before photographing people.</strong> Many Tibetans, especially older pilgrims and monks, do not wish to be photographed. Always ask with a gesture or through your guide. A small offering (CNY 5-10) is appropriate if someone agrees to be photographed.</li>
        <li><strong>Dress modestly in monasteries.</strong> Shoulders and knees should be covered. Remove hats before entering chapels. Avoid shorts and revealing clothing in religious sites. These rules are enforced at Potala Palace, Jokhang Temple, and all monasteries.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not step on thresholds.</strong> When entering a temple or chapel, step over the wooden threshold, not on it. The threshold is considered the boundary between the secular and sacred worlds.</li>
        <li><strong>Never touch a monk's robes or prayer beads.</strong> These are sacred items. Even accidental contact can cause offense.</li>
        <li><strong>Political sensitivity.</strong> Avoid discussing Tibetan political issues, displaying images of the Dalai Lama, or making political statements of any kind. Such actions can result in deportation and a travel ban from China.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Lhasa is generally safe for tourists. Violent crime is rare. The main health concern is altitude. The city has a People's Hospital with basic facilities, but for serious conditions, evacuation to Chengdu or Beijing is necessary. Additional tips: do not drink tap water (bottled water is widely available); Tibetan food is generally safe and delicious; be cautious with street food that has been sitting out; wash hands frequently.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-6 pb-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Common Travel Questions</h2>
    
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-6 py-8">
    <AuthorBio slug="sarah-chen" datePublished="2026-01-20" dateModified="2026-08-06" />
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-6 py-8">
    <div class="altitude-tool">
      <div class="altitude-tool-header">
        <span class="section-tag">Interactive Tool</span>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--color-text)]">Packing List Generator</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Select your travel season, trip length, and activities to get a customized packing checklist.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="packing-check">
        <div class="alt-step" id="pack-step-1">
          <p class="alt-step-q">When are you traveling?</p>
          <div class="alt-step-opts">
            <button class="alt-btn" data-value="winter" onclick="selectPack(this,1)">Winter (Nov-Feb)</button>
            <button class="alt-btn" data-value="shoulder" onclick="selectPack(this,1)">Shoulder (Mar-Apr, Oct)</button>
            <button class="alt-btn" data-value="summer" onclick="selectPack(this,1)">Summer (May-Sep)</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="alt-step" id="pack-step-2" style="display:none">
          <p class="alt-step-q">How many days is your trip?</p>
          <div class="alt-step-opts">
            <button class="alt-btn" data-value="3" onclick="selectPack(this,2)">3-4 days (Lhasa only)</button>
            <button class="alt-btn" data-value="7" onclick="selectPack(this,2)">5-7 days (Lhasa + day trips)</button>
            <button class="alt-btn" data-value="10" onclick="selectPack(this,2)">8+ days (extended Tibet)</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="alt-step" id="pack-step-3" style="display:none">
          <p class="alt-step-q">What activities are you planning?</p>
          <div class="alt-step-opts">
            <button class="alt-btn" data-value="cultural" onclick="selectPack(this,3)">Cultural sightseeing</button>
            <button class="alt-btn" data-value="photo" onclick="selectPack(this,3)">Photography focused</button>
            <button class="alt-btn" data-value="trek" onclick="selectPack(this,3)">Hiking / trekking</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div id="pack-result" style="display:none"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <script is:inline>
    const packAnswers = {};
    function selectPack(el, step) {
      const stepDiv = document.getElementById('pack-step-' + step);
      stepDiv.querySelectorAll('.alt-btn').forEach(b => b.classList.remove('selected'));
      el.classList.add('selected');
      packAnswers[step] = el.dataset.value;
      setTimeout(() => {
        stepDiv.style.display = 'none';
        if (step < 3) {
          document.getElementById('pack-step-' + (step + 1)).style.display = 'block';
        } else {
          showPackResult();
        }
      }, 200);
    }
    function showPackResult() {
      const result = document.getElementById('pack-result');
      const items = [];

      // Base layer - always needed
      items.push('<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Clothing & Layers</h3>');
      items.push('<ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-3 text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">');
      items.push('<li>Thermal base layer (top + bottom)</li>');
      items.push('<li>Fleece or wool mid-layer</li>');
      if (packAnswers[1] === 'winter') {
        items.push('<li>Heavy down jacket (rated to -20 C)</li>');
        items.push('<li>Insulated snow pants</li>');
      } else if (packAnswers[1] === 'shoulder') {
        items.push('<li>Light down jacket or insulated vest</li>');
        items.push('<li>Waterproof shell jacket</li>');
      } else {
        items.push('<li>Lightweight fleece or hoodie</li>');
        items.push('<li>Waterproof windbreaker</li>');
      }
      items.push('<li>Comfortable walking pants (2 pairs)</li>');
      items.push('<li>Warm socks (3-4 pairs, wool preferred)</li>');
      items.push('</ul>');

      // Footwear
      items.push('<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Footwear</h3>');
      items.push('<ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-3 text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">');
      if (packAnswers[3] === 'trek') {
        items.push('<li>Waterproof hiking boots (broken in before trip)</li>');
        items.push('<li>Camp shoes or sandals for evenings</li>');
      } else {
        items.push('<li>Comfortable walking shoes with good grip</li>');
      }
      items.push('</ul>');

      // Sun protection
      items.push('<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Sun Protection (critical at 3,700m)</h3>');
      items.push('<ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-3 text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">');
      items.push('<li>SPF 50+ sunscreen</li>');
      items.push('<li>Lip balm with SPF</li>');
      items.push('<li>Wide-brimmed hat</li>');
      items.push('<li>UV-protective sunglasses (category 3 or 4)</li>');
      items.push('</ul>');

      // Health & altitude
      items.push('<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Health & Altitude</h3>');
      items.push('<ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-3 text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">');
      items.push('<li>Ibuprofen or paracetamol for headaches</li>');
      items.push('<li>Diamox (acetazolamide) — consult your doctor</li>');
      items.push('<li>Rehydration salts or electrolyte tablets</li>');
      items.push('<li>Personal prescription medications</li>');
      if (packAnswers[3] === 'trek') {
        items.push('<li>Blister kit (moleskin, antiseptic)</li>');
        items.push('<li>Basic first-aid kit</li>');
      }
      items.push('</ul>');

      // Extras based on days
      items.push('<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Quantity Guide</h3>');
      items.push('<ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-3 text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">');
      const days = parseInt(packAnswers[2] || '3');
      items.push('<li>Underwear: ' + (days + 2) + ' pairs</li>');
      items.push('<li>Socks: ' + (days + 1) + ' pairs</li>');
      items.push('<li>T-shirts/shirts: ' + Math.ceil(days/2) + ' (plan to re-wear layers)</li>');
      items.push('</ul>');

      // Photography extras
      if (packAnswers[3] === 'photo') {
        items.push('<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Photography Gear</h3>');
        items.push('<ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-3 text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">');
        items.push('<li>Extra memory cards (at least 2)</li>');
        items.push('<li>Spare batteries (cold drains them fast)</li>');
        items.push('<li>Lens cleaning cloth</li>');
        items.push('<li>Tripod (lightweight, check drone regulations)</li>');
        items.push('</ul>');
      }

      // Always
      items.push('<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Always Bring</h3>');
      items.push('<ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-3 text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">');
      items.push('<li>Reusable water bottle (at least 1L)</li>');
      items.push('<li>Passport + printed copies of Tibet Travel Permit</li>');
      items.push('<li>Power bank (20,000 mAh minimum)</li>');
      items.push('<li>Universal travel adapter</li>');
      items.push('<li>Tissues and wet wipes (public toilets rarely have toilet paper)</li>');
      items.push('<li>Hand sanitizer</li>');
      items.push('<li>Snacks from home (granola bars, nuts, chocolate)</li>');
      items.push('</ul>');

      result.innerHTML = '<div class="alt-result-box border-l-emerald-500">' + items.join('') + '<button class="alt-btn text-sm inline-block w-auto mr-3 mt-2" onclick="resetPack()">Start over</button><a href="/travel-tips" class="btn-primary text-sm inline-flex">Full travel tips</a></div>';
      result.style.display = 'block';
    }
    function resetPack() {
      Object.keys(packAnswers).forEach(k => delete packAnswers[k]);
      document.querySelectorAll('#packing-check .alt-btn').forEach(b => b.classList.remove('selected'));
      document.getElementById('pack-step-1').style.display = 'block';
      document.getElementById('pack-step-2').style.display = 'none';
      document.getElementById('pack-step-3').style.display = 'none';
      document.getElementById('pack-result').style.display = 'none';
    }
  </script>


    <FaqAccordion faqs={faqs} />
  </section>

  <PageNav prev={{ href: "/photography", label: "Photography" }} next={{ href: "/best-time", label: "Best Time" }} />
</BaseLayout>

<div class="medical-disclaimer"><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This altitude information is for general guidance only. It is not medical advice. Consult your doctor before traveling to high altitude, especially with heart, lung, or other health conditions. Altitude sickness can be life-threatening.</div>
